  <h2>What the product is for</h2>
  <p>Dental Lab Guru is a SaaS product for dental laboratory workflows. I built it as one of our own products, with the engineering work connected directly to the people who use the software and the decisions needed to keep improving it.</p>
  <p>That direct connection changes the feedback loop. The work is not complete when a feature is merged: release decisions, customer questions and the next useful change all belong to the same product.</p>

  <h2>Product and engineering responsibility</h2>
  <p>Building an own product brings product decisions, backend development, cloud infrastructure and frontend work into one connected responsibility. The useful engineering choices are the ones that make the product clearer for its users and easier to change as the work around it evolves.</p>
  <p>I treat the product as an operating service rather than a finished release: the customer context, the implementation and the next product decision all inform one another.</p>

  <h2>Feedback shapes the next change</h2>
  <p>Direct customer feedback helps shape Dental Lab Guru. It keeps product work grounded in the needs of dental labs instead of treating a feature list as the whole problem.</p>
  <p>That approach makes room for a careful balance: improving the workflow that customers need next while preserving the reliability of the product they already use.</p>

  <h2>Evolving the backend</h2>
  <p>As Dental Lab Guru grew, I moved parts of it towards Node.js, EventBridge and event-driven workflows. The scope matters: this was an evolution of selected parts of the product, not a claim that the whole application was rewritten.</p>
  <p>The change reflects the ongoing engineering work behind a SaaS product—making deliberate adjustments while continuing to support the product in use.</p>

  <h2>The work around the product</h2>
  <p>Operating an own product also means making decisions around releases, customer feedback and the next area of work. Those decisions are part of the engineering responsibility because they shape how the product continues to serve its users over time.</p>

